<comment datatype='string'>&lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=EC-NUMBER&amp;object=EC-1.2.7.12&quot;&gt;EC 1.2.7.12, formylmethanofuran dehydrogenase&lt;/a&gt;, catalyzes the reversible conversion of &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CARBON-DIOXIDE&quot;&gt;CO&lt;SUB&gt;2&lt;/SUB&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&amp;object=Methanofurans&quot; title=&quot;Methanofurans are a group of related carbon-carrier cofactors involved in the first step of the methanogenic reduction of &lt;a href=''/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CARBON-DIOXIDE''&gt;CO&lt;SUB&gt;2&lt;/SUB&gt;&lt;/a&gt; (see &lt;a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=PATHWAY&amp;object=METHANOGENESIS-PWY''&gt;methanogenesis from H&lt;SUB&gt;2&lt;/SUB&gt; and CO&lt;SUB&gt;2&lt;/SUB&gt;&lt;/a&gt;). Methanofurans react with &lt;a href=''/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CARBON-DIOXIDE''&gt;CO&lt;SUB&gt;2&lt;/SUB&gt;&lt;/a&gt; to form the carbamate &lt;i&gt;N&lt;/i&gt;-carboxymethanofuran, which is reduced to &lt;a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&amp;object=Formyl-methanofurans''&gt;a formylmethanofuran&lt;/a&gt; by &lt;a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=EC-NUMBER&amp;object=EC-1.2.7.12''&gt;EC 1.2.7.12, formylmethanofuran dehydrogenase&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;#10;&amp;#10;In addition, the cofactor also participates at a later stage of all methanogenesis pathways. These pathways converge at the compound &lt;a href=''/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=Me-CoM''&gt;methyl-CoM&lt;/a&gt;, which is then disproportionated into &lt;a href=''/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CH4''&gt;methane&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=''/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CARBON-DIOXIDE''&gt;CO&lt;SUB&gt;2&lt;/SUB&gt;&lt;/a&gt;; the latter process involves methanofuran (see &lt;a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=PATHWAY&amp;object=PWY-5209''&gt;methyl-coenzyme M oxidation to CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;/a&gt;).&amp;#10;&amp;#10;Methanofuran is also used by methylotrophic bacteria as a coenzyme to oxidize formaldehyde to formate [&lt;a href=''http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/9651254''&gt;Chistoserdova98&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=''http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/11532013''&gt;Pomper01&lt;/a&gt;].&amp;#10;&amp;#10;Methanofurans are the only cofactors known to contain a furan moiety, although furans have been identified in polyketides and fatty acid derived natural products, and are often formed during the heating or oxidation of carbohydrates [&lt;a href=''http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/12013276''&gt;Graham02&lt;/a&gt;].&amp;#10;&amp;#10;The chemical structure of methanofuran varies among different methanogens. At this time (2014) five variants have been characterized:&amp;#10;&lt;a href=''/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CPD-17181''&gt;methanofuran a&lt;/a&gt; (from &lt;a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-145262''&gt;&lt;i&gt;Methanothermobacter thermautotrophicus&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;) [&lt;a href=''http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/1517208''&gt;Eisenreich92&lt;/a&gt;], &lt;a href=''/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CPD-7628''&gt;methanofuran b&lt;/a&gt; (from &lt;a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-2208''&gt;&lt;i&gt;Methanosarcina barkeri&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;) [&lt;a href=''http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/2883935''&gt;Bobik87&lt;/a&gt;], &lt;a href=''/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CPD-7629''&gt;methanofuran c&lt;/a&gt; (from &lt;a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-2173''&gt;&lt;i&gt;Methanobrevibacter smithii&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;) [&lt;a href=''http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/3170480''&gt;White88a&lt;/a&gt;], &lt;a href=''/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=methanofuran-d''&gt;methanofuran d&lt;/a&gt; (from &lt;a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-2190''&gt;&lt;i&gt;Methanocaldococcus jannaschii&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;) [&lt;a href=''http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/24977328''&gt;Miller14&lt;/a&gt;] and &lt;a href=''/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CPD-17182''&gt;methanofuran e&lt;/a&gt; (from &lt;a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-39152''&gt;&lt;i&gt;Methanococcus maripaludis&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-2187''&gt;&lt;i&gt;Methanococcus vannielii&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-155863''&gt;&lt;i&gt;Methanothermococcus okinawensis&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;) [&lt;a href=''http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/25203397''&gt;Allen14&lt;/a&gt;].&amp;#10;&amp;#10;All methanofurans contain the basic core structure of 4-[&lt;i&gt;N&lt;/i&gt;-&amp;gamma;-L-glutamyl-&amp;gamma;-L-glutamyl)-&lt;i&gt;p&lt;/i&gt;-(&amp;beta;-aminoethyl)phenoxymethyl]-2-(aminomethyl)furan, but with different attached side chains.&quot;&gt;methanofuran&lt;/a&gt; to &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&amp;object=Formyl-methanofurans&quot;&gt;formylmethanofuran&lt;/a&gt;, an intermediate in methanogenesis from &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CARBON-DIOXIDE&quot;&gt;CO&lt;SUB&gt;2&lt;/SUB&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.
&lt;p&gt;
&lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-145262&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Methanothermobacter thermautotrophicus&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; contains two such enzymes, a &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=ENZYME&amp;object=CPLX-562&quot;&gt;tungsten-containing enzyme&lt;/a&gt; and a &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=ENZYME&amp;object=CPLX-561&quot;&gt;molybdenum-containing enzyme&lt;/a&gt;. The molybdenum enzyme, encoded by the &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-348&quot;&gt;fwdA&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-347&quot;&gt;fmdB&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-346&quot;&gt;fmdC&lt;/a&gt; genes, is synthesized only when molybdenum is available in the growth medium. The tungsten enzyme, encoded by the &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-348&quot;&gt;fwdA&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-351&quot;&gt;fwdB&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-350&quot;&gt;fwdC&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-349&quot;&gt;fwdD&lt;/a&gt; genes, is synthesized when either tungsten or molybdenum is present.
&lt;p&gt;
The tungsten enzyme contains &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CPD-21374&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;bis&lt;/i&gt;(guanylyl tungstenpterin) cofactor&lt;/a&gt; and iron-sulfur clusters. If the growth
medium contains molybdenum, the tungsten enzyme will contain molybdenum instead of tungsten [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/8575452&quot;&gt;Hochheimer95&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/8954165&quot;&gt;Hochheimer96&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/8161283&quot;&gt;Bertram94a&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/9342247&quot;&gt;Vorholt97&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/8125106&quot;&gt;Bertram94&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/7813470&quot;&gt;Bertram94b&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/9782487&quot;&gt;Thauer98&lt;/a&gt;].</comment>
